WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.525
Consultants are paid for scoping well. A bad scope can burn months.

00:00:04.525 --> 00:00:13.275
Here's a real story, with names changed. Glacier Coolers bought an Orderful setup built on Shopify. Months later, nothing worked.

00:00:13.275 --> 00:00:24.375
On a call, we learned the truth. Their wholesale orders, prices, and stock live in Zoho Inventory. Shopify is retail only. The whole build pointed at the wrong system.

00:00:24.375 --> 00:00:32.075
Think of a doctor. A good doctor asks questions before writing any prescription. Discovery is your set of questions.

00:00:32.075 --> 00:00:39.425
First: where do wholesale orders, prices, stock, shipments, and invoices live today? That's the system of record.

00:00:39.425 --> 00:00:44.675
Who keys documents today, and how long does it take? That's the pain, and the value.

00:00:44.675 --> 00:00:54.400
Which partners, which documents, and through which network? And for the ship notice, where are the B O L, the tracking, and pallet versus carton? Often, nowhere.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:01.475
Which warehouse is the inventory of record? And which dates matter, like busy season or contract deadlines?

00:01:01.475 --> 00:01:11.350
Then, log in and look. In Glacier's Zoho, everything was there except B O L and pallet fields. The fix was three simple custom fields.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:18.450
Next, estimate with the team's rule of thumb, then sanity-check it. And phase the work so value lands early.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:26.475
Phase one: orders, acknowledgments, and inventory, so new orders flow. Phase two: ship notices and invoices.

00:01:26.475 --> 00:01:36.625
Now, the work order. It has eight parts. Background, the why. Objective, the outcome in business words. And in scope: each flow, with bullets and hours.

00:01:36.625 --> 00:01:45.825
Then assumptions and client responsibilities, like access, or who adds the fields. Then out of scope, like other partners or label hardware.

00:01:45.825 --> 00:01:53.425
Then phasing and timeline, an effort summary table, and finally pricing, with an acceptance block for signatures.

00:01:53.425 --> 00:01:58.025
Before you show any price, settle one question: who is the contract with?

00:01:58.025 --> 00:02:06.800
Some clients are Orderful's customers. Then the document says Orderful, shows no LYSI rate, and Orderful sets the client price.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:16.700
Others contract with LYSI directly. Then the work order says Lysi Consulting, and shows our hourly rate, time and materials, billed monthly.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:26.675
The common mistake is a client document under a name the client doesn't know, or an internal rate on a partner's document. Check the commercial setup first.

00:02:26.675 --> 00:02:37.150
To recap: find the real system of record, ask discovery questions, estimate and phase, write the eight-part work order, and settle the contract before the price.

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:40.750
Next, we'll learn to run projects like a consultant.

